    "result_text": "The build passes. `AcceptanceTest` is the only test class in the suite, and both tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe locality derivation now prefers the postcode, falling back to the city table.\n\n**`LocalityResolver.java`** \u2014 Added a postcode-aware overload `resolve(String city, String postcode)`:\n- A present 4-digit postcode in a known region's inclusive range (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099) yields that region directly.\n- When the postcode is absent or in no known range, it falls back to the existing city-to-region table (`Sydney\u2192NSW`, `Melbourne\u2192VIC`, `Brisbane\u2192QLD`), deriving `UNKNOWN` for unknown/null cities.\n- The original single-arg `resolve(String city)` is retained as the city-only fallback, so `PostcodeValidator` (which validates a postcode *against* the city's region) is unaffected.\n\n**`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 The `locality` mapping now passes both fields: `resolve(owner.getCity(), owner.getPostcode())`.\n\n**`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Updated the `locality` field description to document the postcode-first derivation.\n\nThis returns the same region for the known cities (their pinned postcodes sit in the matching range) while disambiguating cities that merely share a name. Both acceptance tests are green:\n- `coreRegionFromPostcodeRange` \u2014 Sydney + postcode 2000 \u2192 NSW\n- `functionalityPostcodePreferredOverCity` \u2014 unknown city + postcode 3000 \u2192 VIC",
